>	I am not absolutely sure of this, but aren't there some reptiles
>that the females lay eggs, and if the aren't fertilized they hatch into
>females, and if they are, they hatch into males? I suppose I could research
>this, but finals are coming the next two days.

Not really.
Certain lizards have only females in their species (I forget which ones),
and they can lay fertilized eggs without the presence of a male, but
there aren't any that can lay unfertilized eggs that will hatch.
Many reptiles, however, can retain sperm - sometimes for several years,
thus being able to lay fertilized eggs several years in a row after mating
once...

